  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 112 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWater vapor and upper air models show southwest flow over the\nregion, as upper ridge sits to our east and an upper trough is\nlocated over the southwestern CONUS this afternoon. 18Z surface \nanalysis shows a frontal boundary stretching from northeastern SD \ndown into central WY, mostly through our forecast area now. \nSatellite shows clear skies over the forecast area. Winds are \nbreezy out of the northeast, with temperatures this afternoon in \nthe mid to upper 60s.\n\nSlightly cooler temperatures today and Monday (though still stick \nabove normal for Oct), given frontal passage over the area. Return\nflow will make for breezy southeasterly winds early Monday \nmorning throughout the day. Tuesday will see the ridge to our east\nbuild a bit, prompting warming temperatures back into the 70s \nbriefly. Another (dry) front passes through Wednesday with some\nbreezy northwest wind attachment behind it, and cooler \ntemperatures again. That is short lived though, as ridge balloons\nup once again, bringing temperatures near 80 for some areas \nThursday and Friday. We may see elevated fire weather conditions \nat times, but forecast afternoon RH's only dip into the 20-40 \npercent range. Next chance for any meaningful precipitation comes \ntowards the weekend. Models show an upper low over the southwest \nCONUS late in the week developing into a wave and sliding \nnortheast into the northern plains over the weekend. Still far \nenough out that there's low confidence in timing or location of \nany precipitation chances.",
